 our our goals for  my first year undergrad course we  communicate our goals very very upfront  for our high school program too um so we  tell students the first and foremost  thing that we want to do is have fun  what's wonderful about this and i'll tie  this to point number five is that we um  we don't assign grades there's no  metric  of success in our program other than  just learning there's not no  deliverables students complete a short  project but in terms of the core content  that they're learning the goal of the  program is solely to learn and to have  fun doing so  so that means we can really challenge  students too because there are no stakes  we don't have students climbing climbing  over each other to try and get a higher  grade so they can move on and get into  the next school it's not something that  we have to to worry about frankly  um  as part of this students usually will  learn a new programming language so we  currently use go we may change that in  the future but we use a language that's  kind of outside of what students  typically do so that students don't come  in and say well i already know how to  program in python because this way  students will learn something new  regardless  we want them to appreciate that  computation is everywhere in biology  that biology is a very much a  computational discipline in 2021 and we  want students to write their own code so  kind of the full cycle of how our  program works is that we present  students with some biological program  problem  we explain to them how we're going to  solve it computationally we then show  how you might do this by breaking it up  into smaller pieces but the students  solve all of those pieces and then put  them back together into a very large  piece of code that they can then apply  to some real biological data set those  real biological data sets are taken from  either  the microbiome of the three rivers in  pittsburgh so we have two rivers that  flow together into a third and the two  rivers are very different from each  other they flow through different sorts  of environments one goes by a lot of  steel mills the other is much more rural  and so we captured on a boat we went out  and got  water samples with our students from  different seasons and we got a lot of  data sets um metagenomics data sets  but we also used the opportunity of the  pandemic to uh look at publicly  available genome data sets for  for sars cove too as well so but our  main topic of focus is in microbiology  and we communicate to students how our  classroom works so just as we  communicate up front our goals we tell  students why we've set up the program in  the way that we have so we talk to them  about active learning and about how if  we can get our students to do as much in  the classroom as possible as opposed to  just listening to me drone on  and receiving information that  regardless of their background  they will see a marked increase  in  what their  outcome is so in their ability  and that often this doesn't feel like  the experience of learning and other  studies have shown that even though  students are objectively learning more  via active learning  they feel like they're learning less and  we tell students that that that feeling  of  uh  internal struggle is what learning feels  like  we  use this sort of research educational  research to inform a transition to our  online classroom so  over the past two years we've had to run  this program remotely to students across  the country  and  we use best practices for this one of  the things that we do partly based off  of student input is  we now have students solve problems in  teams of four or five so at most points  of the program students are working  together to solve some coding challenge  and they're in small groups doing this  and they can code all in one window  that's shared through something called  atom  so they can all see the same coding  window and then they can all run the  code together  so this creates this really nice kind of  group  dynamic where students have input from  teaching assistants and input from us  the instructors as we're roaming around  but they're all working together  constantly  so that even though they're online  they're able to really uh do something  all the time in the program so what is  it that we cover  um  remember i mentioned our three rivers we  ask you know how is it that the bacteria  that are present in these rivers change  over time how are they different from  one river to the other  um how is seasonality important to this  and we use this to to come up with  simple metrics that are the metrics that  are used by biologists but very easy to  explain metrics for quantifying the  diversity of a single bacterial sample  or comparing two bacterial samples and  sprinkle a little machine learning magic  on this to produce  a plot like this one that shows that  seasonality is more important than  location for determining bacterial  diversity in the three rivers  in the second module of the course and  so i've put all the lines of code that  students write or that we have as our  official solutions  um and it shows i think it's to show how  much code that students will write  because typically  when students are solving some coding  challenge we don't  continue until all the groups are  finished we were worried about whether  or not that would be possible given that  many of our students have never  programmed until like two months before  our program when we send them some  preparatory materials but then all of  our groups pretty much solve every  single one of our problems so when we  say total number of lines of code this  is  you know students are writing this much  code and using it to solve biological  examples the next of this module is  about comparing genes whether it's a  database search  and we talk about blast we don't code  the entire blast algorithm but we  discuss how it works but what we do  code are things like smith waterman  sequence alignment and the students all  code it themselves and then they apply  it to something like comparing stars  cove to source code two um so they can  actually see  something that we hear about in the news  which for example which is that the  variation of sars cove is you know  isolated to you know different points  you have a variable variation throughout  the genome so they can see that based  off of the code that they produced we  even write a genome assembler with our  students  which is our largest coding project it's  they're coding as fast as they can go  and we apply it to both simulated and  real data to show that on our simulated  data that has you know some error rate  um our assembler this is a mummer plot  on the left our assembler does really  good job at assembling the source code  to genome it does an okay job on real  data when we cut it loose on real data  and we use this as an opportunity to  show that there's pretty much always  improvements that we can make even after  we write 400 lines of code if we really  wanted a real genome assembler that  would truly work on a viral data set it  might need to be 10 times as long as  that and if we were going to apply it to  bacteria it might need to be a hundred  times as long  but once we can assemble the genome we  can find the genes in it so we have a  short module for students to find genes  and we use this to build a simple  annotation of something like a viral  genome as well as to compare  our gene finder against annotation for a  longer  larger organism and students even with a  simple approach are able to reconstruct  like 80 of the genes um in say a  sequenced uh annotated bacterium  and then in the final module of our  class we produce evolutionary trees and  we apply this to a variety of different  contexts so we look at for example  coronavirus  genomes taken from patients in different  countries to understand the initial  spread of the chronovirus around the  world we also  have students implement a simple version  of cluster  so that this is the actual um alignment  that that came from students code  so that they can start looking through  this you know throughout the course of  the pandemic  and identifying not just identifying the  variance but defining the variance  themselves and then we compare the  student definition against say the cdc's  definition what is it that makes an  alpha variant what is it that makes a  delta variant and then we can allow  students to classify each of our genomes  in some database say the uk patient  genome database to classify each patient  genome  according to which variant it applies to  and this is we just do you know normal  alpha and delta but even with this  simple classifier that we build we can  see the percentages of the alpha variant  go up you know very highly in in early  2021 and then it's completely replaced  with the delta variant so students again  have this feeling that they're really  doing the research themselves there's  nothing that the real biological  researchers did that they couldn't have  done themselves now that they have this  knowledge um finally i i point out you  know we have very very strong teaching  evaluations for our  for our program but there have been some  qualitative feedback that i love to  profile here because it reinforces our  original goals  most students when they learn to program  they learn about it in the context of  math when they think about computer  science they're thinking about google or  facebook or amazon and it's unfortunate  that even though this computational  revolution has happened our students  even very very strong students many of  whom come from very privileged  high school backgrounds have no idea  that programming can be used to solve  very important problems in biology  um  and that you know many of these problems  are very new for example with respect to  the pandemic  they love the collaboration that we have  um  they really appreciate the challenge  uh one student has called called it  grueling this year but then said  something very positive so if we're able  to balance extreme challenge  without frustrating or stressing out our  students then we really know that we're  doing our job and then i always give my  favorite quote is from nia  um who was interviewed by a blog  to discuss her her experience not just  in our program but with the pre-college  program at cmu as a whole  um and it was wonderful to see that  something that we don't tell our  students is that we pair them up based  off of their backgrounds based off of  their geography  making sure that all of our our groups  are are  kind of ethnically and gender diverse  and  uh she  we didn't tell our students this but she  recognized hey i know that they created  our groups to be as diverse as possible  often with different backgrounds strong  biologists or strong computational  students  and that that's what made the teams work  so well um so she was a wonderful  student i should say but um i always try  to end with with her quote i'm happy to  answer any questions i don't know where  we are on time but thank you for  listening  thank you very much for this  presentation we are just very short on  time in fact one minute over time but it  was great to hear about this program we  do have just uh maybe a couple of  questions that we can fit in  uh the first one is do you accept high  school students from different states uh  for example the state of louisiana  and if yes do you have a summer program  that is online or face-to-face and if  you have a link for that  yeah so we we accept students from  around the world when we're in person um  and we're hoping to be in person  next summer so you can find if you go to  our  um  go to our department website or  there's a pre-college carnegie melon if  you just google pre-college carnegie  mellon you'll find all of the programs  so it may be that you're interested you  find yourself interested in something  else and those are easy to find  all right thank you very much and it was  great to hear the presentation we're  hoping to learn more from the links that  you provided thank you
 

